// A FIXTURE, not a reconstruction. The source it cites is invented prose written for this file.
//
// It plants the four structures Tom Stern calls "misreporting" -- Stern, T. (2016) '"Some Third
// Thing": Nietzsche's Words and the Principle of Charity', Journal of Nietzsche Studies 47(2),
// 287-302, at 295-96 -- plus a clean control and an over-long elision. Stern's own illustrations
// are fictional (his n. 28); so is this treatise.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. Three of Stern's four cases QUOTE ACCURATELY. The verbatim checker verifies
// the currency without detecting the debasement, and would return `exact` on all three. The
// expected verdicts below were written before the code that produces them.
//
//   [hedged-knowledge]  exact + a corrective continuation the quotation stops short of
//   [all-drives]        exact + a dropped leading quantifier AND a contrastive continuation
//   [will-to-power]     exact + a gloss term that appears nowhere in the cited file
//   [present-age]       near   -- words replaced inside the span; already caught, kept as proof
//   [clean-control]     exact, SILENT. If anything fires here the checks are too noisy.
//   [far-elision]       exact + the two halves sit ~300 characters and four headings apart

===
title: Misreporting fixture
reconstruction:
    aim: fit
    unit: meaning
    mode: coherence
    strength: ordinary
defaults:
    chapter: "source/treatise.md"
    reviewed: "2026-08-19"
===

# What is claimed {isGroup: true}

[thesis]: The treatise holds that knowledge is unreliable and the drives are various. #core
    {section: "On knowledge", fidelity: "compression"}
    + [hedged-knowledge]: Knowledge might be false.
        {section: "On knowledge", fidelity: "quotation", source: "\"our knowledge might be false\""}
    + [all-drives]: Drives press towards mastery, accumulation and display.
        {section: "On drives", fidelity: "quotation", source: "\"drives press towards mastery, accumulation and display\""}
    + [clean-control]: Every reader who has followed the argument this far will grant the conclusion without further persuasion.
        {section: "On method", fidelity: "quotation", source: "\"Every reader who has followed the argument this far will grant the conclusion without further persuasion\""}
    + [present-age]: In the present age, human beings carry a mixed inheritance.
        {section: "On the age", fidelity: "quotation", source: "\"In the present age, human beings carry the inheritance of many origins\""}

[thesis-two]: The treatise is committed to a doctrine of power. #core
    {section: "On strength", fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", note: "Nothing in the treatise names a doctrine; reading it as one is what makes the strength passage and the drives passage answer to each other."}
    + [bridge]: What a thing expresses itself as is what that thing is.
        {section: "On strength", fidelity: "imputation", warrant: "enthymeme", note: "The step from how strength expresses itself to what strength IS. The treatise never states it and the reading needs it."}
        + [will-to-power]: The will to power expresses itself as command and as overflow.
            {section: "On strength", fidelity: "quotation", source: "\"as command, as overflow\""}
        + [far-elision]: Our knowledge might be false, and human beings carry a mixed inheritance.
            {section: "On knowledge", fidelity: "quotation", source: "\"Our knowledge might be false ... human beings carry the inheritance of many origins\""}
    + [leap]: A treatise's scattered remarks express a single doctrine.
        {section: "On method", fidelity: "imputation"}
